{"resultType":"INFORMATION","answer":"Protein intake guidelines for resistance training typically recommend a higher protein consumption compared to general dietary guidelines. For individuals engaged in resistance training, the recommended protein intake is often around 1.6 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day to support muscle repair and growth. In contrast, general dietary guidelines for protein intake may suggest a lower intake, around 0.8 grams per kilogram of body weight for the average adult.

Regarding resistance exercise frequency, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that adults engage in muscle-strengthening activities on two or more days a week, targeting all major muscle groups. This is a general guideline and does not specify the number of sessions or the volume of training, which can vary based on individual goals and training programs.

In summary, while protein intake for those involved in resistance training is significantly higher to support muscle adaptation, the frequency of resistance training sessions is generally recommended to be at least twice a week, focusing on all major muscle groups. These guidelines are designed to optimize muscle health and overall fitness.","clarificationQuestion":null,"citedChunkKeys":["WHO-PA-SB-2020-VI-05@1.1.0#s1","WHO-PA-SB-2020-VI-05@1.1.0#s4","WHO-PA-SB-2020-VI-01@1.1.0#s2"],"limitations":[],"missingData":[]}

{"resultType":"INFORMATION","answer":"Protein intake guidelines for resistance training typically recommend a higher protein consumption compared to general dietary recommendations. For individuals engaged in resistance training, the common guideline suggests consuming approximately 1.6 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day to support muscle repair and growth. This is significantly higher than the general recommendation for adults, which is around 0.8 grams per kilogram of body weight.

In terms of resistance exercise frequency, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that adults aged 18-64 should engage in muscle-strengthening activities on two or more days a week, targeting all major muscle groups. This aligns with the protein intake guidelines, as higher protein consumption is particularly beneficial for those who are regularly engaging in resistance training to enhance muscle recovery and growth.

Thus, while both protein intake and resistance training frequency are crucial for optimizing physical fitness and muscle health, the protein intake is specifically tailored to support the demands of resistance training, which is more intensive than general physical activity.","clarificationQuestion":null,"citedChunkKeys":["WHO-PA-SB-2020-VI-05@1.1.0#s1","WHO-PA-SB-2020-VI-05@1.1.0#s4","WHO-PA-SB-2020-VI-01@1.1.0#s1"] ,"limitations":[],"missingData":[]}
